-- title:
--   Two translates of the same double cone always intersect
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space and let $V$ be a double cone in $E$, i.e. a set of the form
--   $$V = \tilde V(v,\vartheta)\cup\bigl(-\tilde V(v,\vartheta)\bigr), \qquad \tilde V(v,\vartheta)=\{\,h \ne 0 : \langle v,h\rangle/\|h\| > \cos\vartheta\,\},$$
--   given by a unit axis $v$ and an apex angle $\vartheta \in (0,\pi/2]$. For $x \in E$ write $V[x] = x + V$. Then for **all** $x, y \in E$,
--   $$V[x] \cap V[y] \;\ne\; \varnothing .$$
--
--   Since a double cone opens in both directions along its axis, two translates of it always overlap far out along that axis: any two points of the space admit a common cone-neighbour for a fixed double cone. This is the existence statement behind chaining through an intermediate point — one point $z$ lying in both cones — of which the positive-measure versions used for averaging arguments are strengthenings.
